# HAKEM 2, 2026-08-30 — CARD 2 (B3 triple gate) MUTATED BY HAND.
# Question asked: does the 17-byte `exit 0` stub actually go red?

$ ./native/machine_intact_test.sh record
  ok   - record: the shipped rabadon-gate answers --version (rabadon-gate 0.2.3)
  ok   - record: the machine baseline is on disk (23 subjects)
machine_intact [record]: 6 passed, 0 failed

$ printf '#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n' > native/rabadon-net; chmod 755 native/rabadon-net
stub size: 17 bytes

$ ./native/machine_intact_test.sh          # the verify arm
  FAIL - verify: 1 shipped artifact(s) are stub-sized
  FAIL - verify: 1 shipped artifact(s) are not what they claim to be
  ok   - verify: the shipped rabadon-gate answers --version (rabadon-gate 0.2.3)
  FAIL - verify: a shipped artifact changed during the run - mode/size/hash moved
       < bin	rabadon-net	755	155096	f1b3f7b008d947528027734ee85544fe32466b031ba807372b2c1a0056e74790
       > bin	rabadon-net	755	17	306c6ca7407560340797866e077e053627ad409277d1b9da58106fce4cf717cb
  ok   - verify: $HOME/.claude/settings.json is byte for byte what it was before the run
  ok   - verify: the live brake is exactly as the operator left it (brake-mode watch;brake-enabled ABSENT)
  ok   - verify: the diff above is the complete list of what moved
machine_intact [verify]: 6 passed, 3 failed     EXIT=1

VERDICT: all THREE legs fired independently - the 1024-byte size floor, the
Mach-O magic-number check, and the recorded sha256. The card's claim holds.
Two extra assertions I did not expect and that matter: the suite ALSO certified
that my mutation did not touch the operator's settings.json or her brake, and it
printed the complete list of what moved. That is the B3 damage class closed.

$ cp <backup> native/rabadon-net; chmod 755
$ shasum -a 256 native/rabadon-net
f1b3f7b008d947528027734ee85544fe32466b031ba807372b2c1a0056e74790  native/rabadon-net
  == the pre-mutation baseline, byte for byte. RESTORED.

FOLLOW-UP I MEASURED WITHOUT BEING ASKED: a second `verify` with no fresh
`record` is RED - "no baseline at .../rabadon-machine-intact-<key>.tsv - the
record arm never ran, so this gate measured NOTHING". The verify arm CONSUMES
the manifest. That is by design (machine_intact_test.sh:155-159) and it is the
right design, but it means the suite is only meaningful inside one `make test`.

WHERE THE GATE SITS, AND THE HONEST LIMIT OF IT (asked in the brief):
`make test` runs it FIRST (`record`, Makefile:122) and LAST (`verify`,
Makefile:1127). So it answers "what did THIS RUN do to the operator's machine".
It does NOT and CANNOT answer "is the operator's machine healthy right now",
because nothing runs it outside `make test`. B1 (the lost execute bit) and B3
(the stub) were both inflicted BY a run, so the gate is in the right place for
the damage it was built for. Damage inflicted by anything else - a failed build,
a manual copy, a crashed editor - is still invisible until someone types
`make test`. The point-in-time arm (machine_intact_test.sh:88 `point_in_time`,
which needs no baseline) would answer that question today if it had a caller.
It has none. RECORDED AS THE GAP, not as a failure of the card.
